WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.080
Hey everyone, привет!

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:02.700
Welcome to Privetrosky!

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:06.940
Episode 59, 440 июля 2026 года.

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:10.320
I'm Olya, and we've got some great Russian words to learn today.

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:11.880
Поехали, let's go!

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:15.300
Our word to the day is Solce, Solce, Solce.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:17.300
That is the Russian word for sun.

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:20.740
Picture the sun as a big golden soul warming everything,

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:22.920
because Solce sounds a bit like soul.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:24.940
Repeat after me, Solce.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:26.640
Now listen to it in a sentence.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:28.340
Solce светит ярко.

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:30.380
That means the sun shines brightly.

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:32.020
Great job repeating that one.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:34.500
Today we are exploring weather words,

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:36.840
so let us build more vocabulary together.

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:41.360
The next word is WETER, WETER, WETTER, that means wind.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:46.580
WETER sounds like WETER, so picture a tough old wind that has blown for years.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:48.380
Repeat after me, WETER.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:50.040
Here is a sentence with it.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:51.220
WETER дует сильно.

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:53.040
The wind blows strongly.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:55.500
Our next word is DOZ-D.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:56.540
ДОСТЬ.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:57.260
DOZ-D.

00:00:57.620 --> 00:00:58.520
That means RAIN.

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:01.860
It sounds a bit like DRENCH, which RAIN certainly does.

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:03.660
Repeat after me, DOZ-D.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:04.840
Listen to this sentence.

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:05.860
DOZ-D идет.

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:07.000
It is RAINING.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:08.460
Now we meet SNEC.

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:09.120
SNEC.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:09.780
SNEC.

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:10.820
That means SNOW.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:14.780
SNEC rhymes with SNEC, so imagine Snowflakes tickering your neck.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:15.980
Repeat after me.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:16.540
SNEC.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:17.880
Here is how we use it.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:19.520
SNEC белый и холодный.

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:21.000
SNOW is white and cold.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:22.860
Next comes ABLACO.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:23.520
ABLACO.

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:24.280
ABLACO.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:25.220
That means cloud.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:30.620
ABLACO sounds like optical, because clouds can block the sun like an optical in the sky.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:31.980
Repeat after me.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:32.800
ABLACO.

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:33.760
Try this sentence.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:35.560
ABLACO play out by nibble.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:37.240
The cloud floats across the sky.

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:38.860
Now we learn TEPLO.

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:39.440
TEPLO.

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:40.220
TEPLO.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.060
That means warm or warm.

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:45.560
TEPLO sounds like TEPIT, but it means pleasantly warm.

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:46.860
Repeat after me.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:47.480
TEPLO.

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:49.120
Here is a simple sentence.

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:50.280
TEPLO.

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:51.420
It is warm today.

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:53.420
Our next word is cold.

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:54.620
холодно.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:55.000
How?

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:55.780
LODINA.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:57.160
That means cold.

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:00.680
Холодно starts exactly like cold, so it is an easy match.

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:01.660
Repeat after me.

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:02.460
холодно.

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:03.860
Listen to the sentence.

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:05.380
На улице холодно.

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:06.660
It is cold outside.

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:08.400
Now we have GRASA.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:09.080
GRASA.

00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:09.680
GRASA.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:11.060
That means standard storm.

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:15.520
GRASA sounds like gross storm, because it grows louder and louder.

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:16.680
Repeat after me.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:17.360
GRASA.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:19.340
Here is how Russian say it.

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:21.820
GRASA идет от understormа с камень.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:23.680
Finally, we reach Raduga.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:24.540
Raduga.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:25.480
Raduga.

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:26.880
That means rainbow.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:29.840
Raduga sounds like rad plus uga.

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:31.280
A happy arc in the sky.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:32.340
Repeat after me.

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:33.000
Raduga.

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:34.340
Tread this sentence.

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:36.540
После дождя появляется Raduga.

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:38.660
After the rain, a rainbow appears.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:42.240
Now let us look at one gram idea using these weather wards.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:46.440
In Russian weather often uses short phrases with no subject at all.

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:49.420
Сегодня тепло simply means today is warm.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:51.760
Was the heat understood but never spoken?

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:55.660
Compare that to English, where we always say it is warm.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.920
You can now say, холодно, to mean it is cold.

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:01.700
Or, дождь идет, to mean it is raining.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:04.540
Or, ветер сильный, to mean the wind is strong.

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:07.420
That feels natural once you try it a few times.

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:11.440
Russian love talking about the weather because the huge country has dramatic changes.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:15.920
Snowy winters in Siberia contrasts with sunny summers in the south.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:19.920
People often greet each other with как погода, to ask how the weather is,

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:21.600
as a friendly way to start a chat.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:23.400
Now it is practice time.

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:28.300
For kids, draw a picture of today's weather and label the sun, clouds, and wind in Russian.

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:30.400
Then say each word out loud.

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:36.140
For adults, step outside or look out the window and describe the weather in one simple Russian sentence,

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:37.280
using today's words.

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:40.860
Now here is something really cool about one of today's words.

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:43.240
Want to know a secret about weather?

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:48.240
Did you know that the Russian word ветер and the English word wind are actually cousins

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:49.760
from the same ancient root?

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:53.400
They both come from an old language root meaning to blow.

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:57.360
That root travelled through Germanic tongues to become English wind

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:00.700
and through Slavic branches to become Russian ветер.

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:05.040
Be careful though, because ветер sounds nothing like the false-friend weather,

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:07.360
which in Russian is actually погода.

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:12.100
Next time you feel the wind on your face, remember it is cousins with ветер.

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:14.400
They both come from the same blowing root.

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:18.220
And that connection is your secret shortcut to remember it forever.

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:20.480
Maladets, that means well done.

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:25.540
Remember every expert started as a beginner practicing today's words out loud,

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:28.660
even just once and you'll be amazed how fast you learn.

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:29.920
See you next time.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:30.300
Пока!

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:32.040
That's Russian for bye.

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:36.060
And if you'd rather watch then listen, find us on YouTube at NERA Are You.

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:37.680
Links in the show notes.

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:40.480
This release sounds very helpful and your voice synthesis.

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:43.680
The selection and analysis will remain with a person.
